  { name: 'Brown-necked Raven', url: '/species/brown-necked-raven/' },
  { name: 'Diet',              url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'What does the Brown-necked Raven eat?', a: 'The Brown-necked Raven is an omnivore adapted to arid and desert habitats. Its diet includes invertebrates (beetles, locusts, termites), lizards and other small reptiles, carrion, eggs, grain, dates, and food waste from oases and settlements. In the Sahara and Arabian deserts, the species can go long periods between food sources — it is an efficient opportunist adapted to the feast-or-famine nature of desert foraging.' },
  { q: 'How does the Brown-necked Raven survive in the desert?', a: 'The Brown-necked Raven is physiologically and behaviourally adapted to desert conditions. It can tolerate high temperatures and reduced water availability — getting much of its moisture from food rather than standing water. It exploits seasonal food pulses (locust swarms, termite eruptions) when they occur, and relies on carrion and stored invertebrates during leaner periods. It is closely associated with human settlements (oases, villages, roads) which provide reliable supplementary food.' },
  { q: 'Does the Brown-necked Raven eat dates?', a: 'Yes. Date palms (Phoenix dactylifera) are common in oases across the Brown-necked Raven\'s range, and the species is documented raiding date crops — eating ripe dates and picking through fallen fruit. This can make it an agricultural pest in oasis farming communities. Dates provide energy-rich food during periods when invertebrates and carrion are scarce.' },
];
