    <h1>Brown-necked Raven Diet: What This Species Eats</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Brown-necked Raven (<em>Corvus ruficollis</em>) is a desert-adapted omnivore. Its diet spans invertebrates (especially beetles and locusts), small reptiles, carrion, eggs, grain, dates, and food from human settlements. The species is well-adapted to the irregular, feast-or-famine food availability of Saharan and Middle Eastern deserts, exploiting seasonal pulses of insects and reliable food near oases and roads.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Primary Food Items</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Based on HBW species account and published Saharan ornithological records:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Invertebrates:</strong> beetles (particularly dung beetles and tenebrionids), locusts, grasshoppers, and termites. Locust swarms are an important periodic food source; the species tracks locust eruptions and aggregates around them in large numbers.</li>
      <li><strong>Lizards and reptiles:</strong> small lizards (particularly skinks and lacertids) are an important protein source in desert habitats where mammal prey is scarce. The species is agile enough to catch lizards in open terrain.</li>
      <li><strong>Carrion:</strong> roadkill (camels, goats, donkeys) and other carcasses. Roads through the Sahara and Arabian deserts are important foraging corridors for the species.</li>
      <li><strong>Eggs:</strong> eggs of ground-nesting desert birds — larks, wheatears, sandgrouse.</li>
      <li><strong>Grain and seeds:</strong> sorghum, millet, and other grains in oasis agricultural settings; native seeds.</li>
      <li><strong>Fruit:</strong> dates from Phoenix dactylifera and other desert fruits.</li>
      <li><strong>Human food waste:</strong> oasis settlements, desert roads, military posts — human food waste is reliable and sought out.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Desert Foraging Adaptations</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Brown-necked Raven's diet reflects specific adaptations to desert foraging conditions:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Water economy:</strong> the species obtains much of its water from prey (lizards, insects, carrion) rather than relying on standing water. This independence from water sources allows it to inhabit areas far from permanent water.</li>
      <li><strong>Tracking seasonal pulses:</strong> the species shows some nomadic movement to exploit temporary food abundance — locust swarms, rainfall-triggered insect eruptions, termite flights. This mobility is essential in an environment where food is spatially and temporally unpredictable.</li>
      <li><strong>Road association:</strong> desert roads provide both roadkill and edges where food concentrates. Brown-necked Ravens are frequently seen along roadsides throughout North Africa and the Middle East.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

